#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""auditorium-emergency — emergency mode when /data is unavailable, an update
cannot recover, or /data is nearly full (§4.6, §16.7, §14.5, Q12).

Thin CLI: everything this does lives in auditorium_emergency.py
(/usr/local/lib/auditorium on the built image), which is deliberately
importable on its own — plain standard library, nothing from proskenion —
so it is unit-tested without a systemd, an nginx or a real /data.

Three modes, matching the three units that run this file:

  auditorium-emergency --clear-stale   auditorium-emergency-cleanup.service's
                                   ExecStart, unconditional and first in boot
                                   order: remove a reason file left by a
                                   previous, now-resolved entry, so exiting
                                   emergency mode by rebooting (§4.6) is
                                   actually automatic rather than gated on a
                                   file nothing ever removes.

  auditorium-emergency --detect   auditorium-emergency-detect.service's
                                   ExecStart: decide data_unavailable or
                                   data_readonly and write the reason file.
                                   Runs only when that unit's own
                                   ConditionPathIsReadWrite=!/data has passed.

  auditorium-emergency [--port N] auditorium-emergency.service's ExecStart:
                                   read the reason file (falling back to
                                   detecting it live if one is somehow
                                   missing), switch nginx to emergency.conf,
                                   send the one alert this entry gets, and
                                   serve /health and the page until the
                                   machine reboots — exit is automatic (§4.6),
                                   there is no "clear emergency mode" step.

migration_failed and disk_full never reach this file directly: they are
written by auditorium-update-rollback, which starts
auditorium-emergency.service once the reason is already on disk.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import sys

sys.path.insert(0, "/usr/local/lib/auditorium")

import auditorium_emergency  # noqa: E402

if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(auditorium_emergency.main(sys.argv[1:]))
